course: geography
lesson: 6
title: Islands, seas and lakes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An island is land with water all round; the biggest is Greenland, because Australia is counted as a continent instead.
explain: A sea is a smaller part of the ocean, often half-closed by land, like the Mediterranean of Venice and Genoa or the Baltic of the Hanseatic towns you trade in EU5.
explain: How salty a sea is depends on a simple balance: lots of rivers make it fresher, like the Baltic, and strong sun and few rivers make it saltier, like the Mediterranean.
explain: A lake is water closed in by land, and the Caspian is so huge and salty that people call it a sea, while Baikal is so deep it holds more water than all five Great Lakes.
explain: Water can also vanish: the Aral Sea shrank to about a tenth because its rivers were taken for farms, and boats now lie in the desert.
fact: Greenland is the world's largest island, with about 2.2 million square kilometres, and Australia is not counted because it is called a continent.
fact: New Guinea is the second largest island and Borneo the third, and Borneo is shared by three countries: Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei.
fact: Great Britain is the largest island in Europe and the ninth largest in the world.
fact: Indonesia is the world's largest country made only of islands, with more than 17,000 islands; many are tiny rocks that the tide covers and uncovers, so the exact count is hard.
fact: A sea is a smaller part of the ocean, often partly enclosed by land, such as the Mediterranean, the Baltic or the North Sea.
fact: The Caspian Sea is the world's largest lake by area, at about 370,000 square kilometres, and its water is salty, though only about a third as salty as the ocean; rivers such as the Volga flow in, but none flows out, so water leaves only by evaporating and the salt stays behind.
fact: Lake Baikal in Siberia is the deepest lake on Earth at about 1,640 metres, the oldest at about 25 million years, and holds about a fifth of the world's unfrozen fresh surface water.
fact: Lake Superior, between Canada and the United States, is the largest freshwater lake by area, at about 82,000 square kilometres.
fact: The Mediterranean is an almost closed sea, and more water evaporates from it than rivers and rain bring in, so ocean water keeps flowing in from the Atlantic.
fact: The Baltic Sea is brackish, much less salty than the ocean, because many rivers pour fresh water into it and its only exit is through narrow Danish straits.
fact: The shore of the Dead Sea, between Israel, the West Bank and Jordan, is about 430 metres below sea level, the lowest land on Earth, and its water is almost ten times saltier than the ocean, so heavy (dense) that people float on it easily.
fact: The Aral Sea in Central Asia, once the fourth largest lake in the world, has shrunk to about a tenth of its former size since the 1960s.
fact: Greenland today belongs to the Kingdom of Denmark, but in 1337, when EU5 starts, its Norse settlers and Iceland were under Norway, which joined Denmark in the Kalmar Union only in 1397.
exercise: Which is the world's largest island? | Greenland | New Guinea | Great Britain | Madagascar
exercise: Why do geographers not call Australia the largest island? | It is counted as a continent | It is smaller than Greenland | It is not surrounded by water | It is made of many islands
exercise: Which three countries share Borneo? | Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei | Indonesia, Thailand and Brunei | Malaysia, the Philippines and Brunei | Indonesia, Malaysia and Singapore
exercise: Which is the second largest island in the world? | New Guinea | Borneo | Great Britain | Iceland
exercise: Which is the largest island in Europe? | Great Britain | Ireland | Iceland | Sicily, in the Mediterranean
exercise: About how many islands does Indonesia have? | More than 17,000 | About 170 big ones | About 1,700 | Exactly 7, all very large
exercise: What makes the North Sea a sea and not an ocean? | It is a smaller part of the ocean, partly enclosed by land | It has fresh water | It has no tides at all, unlike the oceans around it | It is shallower than a lake
exercise: Which is the largest lake in the world by area? | The Caspian Sea | Lake Superior in North America | Lake Baikal | Lake Victoria
exercise: How salty is the Caspian Sea compared with the ocean? | About a third as salty | Ten times saltier than the ocean | Exactly as salty | Completely fresh
exercise: Which is the deepest lake on Earth? | Lake Baikal | Lake Superior | Loch Ness in Scotland | The Caspian Sea
exercise: How old is Lake Baikal? | About 25 million years | About 2,500 years | About 10,000 years, since the last Ice Age | About 4 billion years
exercise: Which is the largest freshwater lake by area? | Lake Superior | Lake Baikal | The Caspian Sea | The Dead Sea
exercise: Why does Atlantic water keep flowing into the Mediterranean? | More water evaporates from it than rivers and rain bring | The Mediterranean is lower than the ocean by 1 km | Ships pump it in | Rivers push water out of it and the ocean refills it from the Black Sea
exercise: Why is the Baltic Sea brackish? | Many rivers pour in and its exit is narrow | It is fed by melting icebergs only | It is a salt lake | The salt was all washed out of it by long cold winters
exercise: Where is the lowest land on Earth? | The shore of the Dead Sea | The bottom of Death Valley | The polders of the Netherlands | The shore of the Caspian Sea
exercise: What has happened to the Aral Sea since the 1960s? | It has shrunk to about a tenth of its size | It has doubled in size | It has frozen solid | It has joined up with the Caspian through a new river
exercise: Which country does Greenland belong to today? | The Kingdom of Denmark | Norway | Canada, its nearest neighbour | Iceland
test: In EU5 you see Denmark and Greenland together in 1337. What did the real map look like? | Greenland's settlers and Iceland were under Norway, not yet Denmark | Denmark already ruled Greenland and Norway | Greenland belonged to England | Nobody lived in Greenland at all
test: Which island in Europe is bigger than Ireland and Iceland? | Great Britain | Sicily | Corsica | Crete
test: Why is it hard to count exactly how many islands Indonesia has? | Some are tiny rocks and tides cover and uncover them | Indonesia keeps the number secret | The islands drift about and move every year | Most of them are under ice
test: How can Baikal be smaller in area than Lake Superior but hold more water? | It is far deeper | Its water is heavier | It is fed by the sea | It is measured in winter only
test: Why is the Caspian salty even though fresh rivers flow into it? | Water leaves only by evaporation, leaving the salt behind | The Volga is a salty river | The ocean flows into it secretly under the ground | Salt mines on the shore keep falling into the water
test: Why do people float so easily in the Dead Sea? | Its very salty water is denser than normal water | The water is warm | There are no waves at all to pull you under | The lake is very shallow
test: Why might some freshwater fish survive in parts of the Baltic? | Its water is only a little salty, especially in the north | It has no salt at all | Fish are carried in by ships | The Baltic is really a lake cut off from the North Sea
test: Mate says the Med and the Atlantic are equally salty cos they're joined. True? | No, the Mediterranean is saltier because of strong evaporation | Yes, joined seas are always the same | No, the Mediterranean is fresh | Yes, the Strait of Gibraltar is wide enough to mix the two of them fully
test: What lesson does the Aral Sea teach about taking water from rivers? | Take too much and a whole sea can die | Rivers always refill a lake | Lakes grow when farms use water | Deserts make more rain
test: Which is bigger in area, the Caspian Sea or Lake Superior? | The Caspian Sea, by far | Lake Superior, by far | They are about the same size | Nobody knows
test: What would happen to the Baltic if its rivers dried up but the straits stayed open? | It would grow saltier, closer to the ocean | It would turn into fresh water | It would get colder for ever | Nothing would change at all
exam: Identify the largest body of water upon the Earth's surface that is wholly enclosed by land. | The Caspian Sea | Lake Superior | Lake Baikal | The Lakes Michigan and Huron taken together
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the salinity of the Baltic Sea? | It is brackish owing to abundant river inflow and restricted outflow | It exceeds that of the Dead Sea | It is identical to that of the open ocean on account of the Danish straits | It is entirely fresh
exam: Determine which island, Australia being excluded as a continent, possesses the greatest area. | Greenland | New Guinea | Borneo, in Asia | Great Britain
exam: Ascertain the principal cause of the diminution of the Aral Sea since the nineteen-sixties. | The diversion of its feeder rivers for irrigation | A succession of earthquakes | The rising of the ocean | The draining of its waters by a canal constructed to the Caspian
